No.

Two of clubs?

I shall explain. First: don't walk around burdened. Just don't. Unless
you are moving a specific heavy object to a nearby point, have a
known-safe path, and are prepared to drop your load at a moment's
notice, it's a bad idea. (Example: Carrying a heavy corpse to an altar
one room away.)

If that means you must make hard choices about what to carry, then
make those choices. "Carry more stuff and be burdened" is never a good
answer.

For things you don't need at a moment's notice, make a stash. A chest
or box is a good choice - monsters will not mess with the contents.
Carry only what you need all the time, or what you will need in a
hurry. (A lizard corpse does you no good in a stash.)

Scrolls and potions should always be in a container (in your inventory
or otherwise); it is too easy for them to get destroyed otherwise. I
can see an exception for one !oFH, and maybe one (c)?oT if you have no
other quick escape mechanism (e.g. /oT, /oDig).

Personally testing anything of unknown bless/curse (BUC) status is
almost suicidal. Do it only in extreme emergencies. Drop items on
altars if you don't know their status.

Trying unknown !?=/ is risky, but you can mitigate the risk. Part of
that is knowing what the nasty possibilities are, and taking steps to
avoid or eliminate those. Scrolls (or spellbooks) of identify are
great, but there are ways to ID (or at least greatly narrow down the
options for) !?=/ short of trying them.

Take potions for example. You get clear=water for free. Dipping a
dart in unknown potions will reveal poison and polymorph, two of the
really bad ones. A unihorn will convert most of the other unpleasant
ones to water (or cure their effects if you drink them). That leaves
acid (a minor nuisance once you grow a few HP) and sleep (test inside
a small, safe locked room). I usually stockpile potions until I get
a unihorn, dip darts to find poison/poly, try the rest in a safe area,
and convert "bad" potions to holy water. (Never test cursed potions,
or anything else cursed.)

In general, 1) know what's the worst that can happen and 2) have a
plan for how to deal with it if it happens. If one of the
possibilities can cause you serious trouble and you don't have an
easy remedy at hand, leave it alone until you do.

